#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186384391 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f87bc14d-5e68-5a5e-bec8-f1b5b21c02e8" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Arc Length of a Smooth Curve Using the Arc Length Formula"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "de481db3-c405-5a31-8a80-acd98c2f1a9c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{8}\\operatorname{ln}⁡4-\\operatorname{ln}⁡4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384393} {:answer/id #uuid "b00cecac-8428-564d-997a-c8a9dcd92e8f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{14}{29}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384394} {:answer/id #uuid "bb4a88bf-7563-502c-8b76-4b411f1d217e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}{4}+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384395} {:answer/id #uuid "07c02ea3-54d2-53c4-97d1-e71d7eebc272" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡4+\\frac{15}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384396} {:answer/id #uuid "640f9827-b5a6-5261-8dde-58b6a8cba530" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡4-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384397}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "07c02ea3-54d2-53c4-97d1-e71d7eebc272" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡4+\\frac{15}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384396} :answer-field/id #uuid "2e388846-ab83-514e-8fda-927ace8262c8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384392}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "430a9509-653c-5419-a6b3-7926dc6bcc6a" :question/math-academy-id "q-5998" :question/problem "Compute the arc length of $y=\\frac{1}{8}{x}^{2}-\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ for $x\\in [1,4].$" :question/worked-solution "The arc length of a curve $y=f(x)$ between $x=a$ and $x=b$ can be found using the formula\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $f(x)=\\frac{1}{8}{x}^{2}-\\operatorname{ln}⁡x,$ $a=1,$ and $b=4.$\n\nFirst, we find the derivative of $f(x):$\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=\\frac{1}{4}x-\\frac{1}{x}\n$$\n\nNow, let's compute $\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}} & =\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{1}{4}x-\\frac{1}{x})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+\\frac{1}{16}{x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}-\\frac{1}{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{16}{x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}+\\frac{1}{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+\\frac{16}{{x}^{2}}+8} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\sqrt{{(\\frac{{x}^{2}+4}{x})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{2}+4}{4x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}=\\frac{{x}^{2}+4}{4x}$ into the arc length formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{1}^{4}\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{4}\\frac{{x}^{2}+4}{4x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{4}\\frac{x}{4}+\\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+\\frac{1}{8}{x}^{2})|}_{1}^{4} \\\\  & =[\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4)+\\frac{1}{8}(4)^{2}]-[\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1)+\\frac{1}{8}(1)^{2}] \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡4+2-\\operatorname{ln}⁡1-\\frac{1}{8} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡4+\\frac{15}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
